  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 PM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nOur area for the next couple of days will be wedged between\nsurface high pressure to the north and west and a stationary front\nto the south and east. Behind the departing trough over the\nnortheast, a weak cut-off low will cycle while remaining trapped\nin place, with upper ridging to the west and broad troughing\nencompassing the northern tier of the country.\n\nDuring the afternoon and evening hours, showers and thunderstorm\nchances will increase across the area, with the highest\nprobability over the Southern Appalachians. The main possible\nhazard in places impacted will be, localized flooding under a \nheavier storm or training and wind gusts. A marginal risk for\nsevere storms will primarily be east of the forecast area over \nthe Carolinas tomorrow. Forcing near the front/low, paired with a\nvery moist and unstable airmass with steepening lapse rates, may \nlead to wet microburst potential. This could be possible here \nalong and closest to our eastern border. \n\nWednesday may bring more widespread chances of showers and\nthunderstorms across the region, when the weak upper low is\nforecast to be absorbed by the aforementioned broad trough.\nFollowing that, mostly unchanged conditions the rest of the week\nand into the weekend within weak flow/steering, diurnal showers \nand thunderstorms, humid days, and not so much fluctuating of \ntemperatures.",
